47-1c43a50b43a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499439 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499439 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499439 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499439 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499439 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499439 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Unit Interval for a Root of an Integer" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499439 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186499498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499439 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9f6b9f6c-95f0-5c70-b22d-f60c0e9d20da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499444 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499444 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499444 #edb/ref 1069 "q-555450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499444 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499444 #edb/ref 1071 "The value of $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between two consecutive whole numbers $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499444 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0afb67b2-5531-5190-bc7f-fa3f3f29c54e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499444 #edb/ref 1074 "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $57.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $57$ are $49$ and $64.$ We have\n\n$$\n49<57<64.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{49} & <\\sqrt{57}<\\sqrt{64} \\\\ 7 & <\\sqrt{57}<8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2b5ce9c24d223674d04686a577347e43cd40728194596e75df7c0faa006d2888.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $7$ and $8.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=7$ and $b=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499445 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499445 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556442" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499445 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499445 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following irrational numbers is the greatest?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499445 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f4f8da7-ea6d-5fee-9cf3-3eb27646ae68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499445 #edb/ref 1074 "To identify the greatest irrational number, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each root lies.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{250}$ lies between $6$ and $7,$ since ${6}^{3}=216$ and ${7}^{3}=343.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{220}$ lies between $6$ and $7,$ since ${6}^{3}=216$ and ${7}^{3}=343.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{35}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{28}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{100}$ lies between $4$ and $5,$ since ${4}^{3}=64$ and ${5}^{3}=125.$\n\nComparing these bounds, the greatest number must be between $6$ and $7.$ So, it is either $\\sqrt[3]{250}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{220}.$\n\nSince $250>220,$ we know that $\\sqrt[3]{250}>\\sqrt[3]{220}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{250}$ is the greatest number among the given options." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499447 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499447 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499447 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556446" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499447 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499447 #edb/ref 1071 "The value of $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies between two consecutive tenths $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499447 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a16d859-8fe1-5bbc-a36b-6b57bd9a46c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499447 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $50$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies.\nWe know that ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${4}^{3}=64$.\nSince $27<50<64$, we know that $3<\\sqrt[3]{50}<4$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $3$ and $4$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3.5}^{3} & =42.875 \\\\ {3.6}^{3} & =46.656 \\\\ {3.7}^{3} & =50.653 \\\\ {3.8}^{3} & =54.872\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $46.656<50<50.653$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{3.6}^{3}<50<{3.7}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n3.6<\\sqrt[3]{50}<3.7\n$$\n\nSo, $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies between $3.6$ and $3.7$. Since we are given that $a<b$, we have $a=3.6$ and $b=3.7$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499453 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499453 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556431" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499453 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499453 #edb/ref 1071 "Between which two consecutive whole numbers does $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ lie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499453 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "283f891f-b99b-51a0-abad-92ec462820ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499453 #edb/ref 1074 "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ lies between, we can compare $20$ to nearby perfect cubes.\n\n- The closest perfect cube less than $20$ is $8,$ since ${2}^{3}=8.$\n\n- The closest perfect cube greater than $20$ is $27,$ since ${3}^{3}=27.$\n\nThis means $20$ is between $8$ and $27:$\n\n$$\n8<20<27\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{8} & <\\sqrt[3]{20}<\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\ 2 & <\\sqrt[3]{20}<3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ lies between $2$ and $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499463 #edb/ref 1069 "e-51599" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921864