ref 1069 "q-556447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499466 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499466 #edb/ref 1071 "The value of $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies between two consecutive tenths $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499466 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4cff9fda-568d-5f3b-ab6b-f2c93ac02430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499466 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $10$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies.\nWe know that ${2}^{3}=8$ and ${3}^{3}=27$.\nSince $8<10<27$, we know that $2<\\sqrt[3]{10}<3$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $2$ and $3$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2.0}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2.1}^{3} & =9.261 \\\\ {2.2}^{3} & =10.648 \\\\ {2.3}^{3} & =12.167\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $9.261<10<10.648$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{2.1}^{3}<10<{2.2}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n2.1<\\sqrt[3]{10}<2.2\n$$\n\nSo, $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies between $2.1$ and $2.2$. Since we are given that $a<b$, we have $a=2.1$ and $b=2.2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499468 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499468 #edb/ref 1069 "q-555452" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499468 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499468 #edb/ref 1071 "Between which two consecutive whole numbers does $\\sqrt{40}$ lie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499468 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f73f19a-494d-5194-ba7a-fdcda5923013" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499468 #edb/ref 1074 "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{40}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $40.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $40$ are $36$ and $49.$ We have\n\n$$\n36<40<49.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36} & <\\sqrt{40}<\\sqrt{49} \\\\ 6 & <\\sqrt{40}<7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/5c/5c2775762ff274451dfd3258bacbede7240e6bad25b782cc86a76484e61d31bb.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{40}$ lies between $6$ and $7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499478 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499478 #edb/ref 1069 "q-555465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499478 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499478 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the best estimate for $\\sqrt[3]{85}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499478 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ccb9537-59db-525f-88c4-f6dfbe10d934" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499478 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt[3]{85},$ we first locate the perfect cubes that are closest to $85.$\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{3} & =64 \\\\ {5}^{3} & =125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $85$ is between $64$ and $125,$ its cube root must be between $4$ and $5.$ This eliminates any choices that do not fall in this range.\n\n![](images/9b/9b97872f5a4258a5b417f6e91bfc33733125b7b0f16299b6cddf06ce3f6cd87b.png)\n\nWe can further refine our estimate by considering distances. The number $85$ is closer to $64$ than it is to $125,$ so we expect its cube root to be closer to $4$ than to $5.$ \n\nLooking at the remaining choices—$4.1,$ $4.4,$ and $4.7$—we can test a value by cubing it to see how close the result is to $85.$ Let's test $4.4$ by cubing it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4.4}^{3} & =4.4\\cdot 4.4\\cdot 4.4 \\\\  & =19.36\\cdot 4.4 \\\\  & =85.184\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $85.184$ is very close to $85,$ we can confidently choose $4.4$ as our estimate.\n\nTherefore, the best estimate for $\\sqrt[3]{85}$ is $4.4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556439" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499481 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the best estimate for $\\sqrt{73}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ec75543-e826-5114-b0c0-b95399a3601d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499481 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt{73},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $73.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $64={8}^{2}$ and $81={9}^{2}.$\n\nSince $64<73<81,$ we know that the square root of $73$ lies between $8$ and $9.$ This tells us that $7.6$ and $36.5$ are incorrect estimates.\n\nBecause $73$ is slightly closer to $81$ than to $64$ (the distance to $64$ is $9,$ and the distance to $81$ is $8$), we expect $\\sqrt{73}$ to be near the middle of the interval.\n\nWe can test the plausible choices by squaring them to see which one results in a value closest to $73:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8.2}^{2} & =67.24 \\\\ {8.5}^{2} & =72.25 \\\\ {8.9}^{2} & =79.21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${8.5}^{2}=72.25$ is very close to $73.$\n\nTherefore, the best estimate for $\\sqrt{73}$ is $8.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556452" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499488 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the numbers $\\sqrt{20},$ $\\sqrt{5},$ and $\\sqrt{11}.$ Write these three numbers in order from least to greatest.\n\nThe numbers, in order from least to greatest, are:\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c62dfe4-3d3e-58f1-bc84-569e3d239868" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499488 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find two consecutive whole numbers that bracket each root. We do this by finding the perfect squares just below and just above the number under the radical.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{5}:$ \nSince $4<5<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{5}<\\sqrt{9}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt{5}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{11}:$\nSince $9<11<16,$ we have $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{11}<\\sqrt{16}.$ This means $3<\\sqrt{11}<4.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{20}:$\nSince $16<20<25,$ we have $\\sqrt{16}<\\sqrt{20}<\\sqrt{25}.$ This means $4<\\sqrt{20}<5.$\n\nFrom these bounds, we can see that all three numbers fall into different unit intervals:\n\n$$\n2<\\sqrt{5}<3<\\sqrt{11}<4<\\sqrt{20}<5\n$$\n\nComparing these intervals, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}<\\sqrt{11}<\\sqrt{20}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the numbers in order from least to greatest are $\\sqrt{5},$ $\\sqrt{11},$ and $\\sqrt{20}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499489 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556440" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499489 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499489 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the best estimate for $\\sqrt{29}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f1f35ac-b2a3-5225-badd-5521e9e9745c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499489 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt{29},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $29.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $25={5}^{2}$ and $36={6}^{2}.$\n\nSince $25<29<36,$ we know that the square root of $29$ lies between $5$ and $6.$ This tells us that $4.9$ and $14.5$ are incorrect estimates.\n\nBecause $29$ is much closer to $25$ than to $36$ (the distance to $25$ is $4,$ and the distance to $36$ is $7$), we expect $\\sqrt{29}$ to be near the lower end of the interval.\n\nWe can test the plausible choices by squaring them to see which one results in a value closest to $29:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.4}^{2} & =29.16 \\\\ {5.7}^{2} & =32.49 \\\\ {5.9}^{2} & =34.81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${5.4}^{2}=29.16$ is very close to $29.$\n\nTherefore, the best estimate for $\\sqrt{29}$ is $5.4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499495 #edb/ref 1069 "e-51600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499495 #edb/ref 1071 "Between which two consecutive tenths does ![](images/4b/4be23abb7ad197619ea40d11d47cf1fdbb147bfdb686ebb3ec3edf35fe86a11a.svg) lie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499495 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "809623d1-30aa-5672-ac4f-f3c539a51eb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499495 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which ![](images/4b/4be23abb7ad197619ea40d11d47cf1fdbb147bfdb686ebb3ec3edf35fe86a11a.svg) lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket ![](images/c4/c470a153cbef9a736d6098e83a3cfe29bd8aab79763d40e7d037249050243a38.svg).\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers ![](images/4b/4be23abb7ad197619ea40d11d47cf1fdbb147bfdb686ebb3ec3edf35fe86a11a.svg) lies.\nWe know that ![](images/0a/0a53cb6ef00f2cee34191c318c35c03772be8c31188a3d08a3934c0bd7bcecc1.svg) and ![](images/99/99407afccad5e3e8923e1f7baefd9c2c39a0527a7891c4fbeed2f278831675b3.svg).\nSince ![](images/52/5283496580d408c3985a17e58b695eff54043bfcaaee8f2289f795ee2bb3383e.svg), we know that ![](images/88/88c76039d22e912b07971b62a9f1625d144396596eb04a37713b2b8d5145a9a8.svg).\n\nNext, we can test decimals between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) to narrow down the interval:\n![](images/73/732e30063191974603e50ade6b2d95bbe7064d4a29d1a03f607f73b65545e3e4.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/7e/7ed21dc216d8118e893b07314d80a792d90411459ee9ea6f57de2ddbdb9a2f60.svg), we can write:\n![](images/79/79e2114a0d701bc50c5b8296a1379cc1e86348678c137f32b59b16f3ad40530a.svg)\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n![](images/c6/c6f904b992c68db63b37b809d99a8f385