mages/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/85/85ab4d14e4ae710116ac84de4b03b3cf35723ab969b7abfba97889c497974147.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499506 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499506 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499506 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499506 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499506 #edb/ref 1071 "The value of $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between two consecutive whole numbers $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499506 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b525db8-241b-5276-b6b9-4b8689047d2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499506 #edb/ref 1074 "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $110.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $110$ are $100$ and $121.$ We have\n\n$$\n100<110<121.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{100} & <\\sqrt{110}<\\sqrt{121} \\\\ 10 & <\\sqrt{110}<11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/9b/9bef06cb0000173c30147f4f9f633f3adec207b688adb005bcf0fff1aa8d7b13.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $10$ and $11.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=10$ and $b=11.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499507 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499507 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499507 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499507 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the value of $\\sqrt[3]{20},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{20}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499507 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b9de470-595d-5301-8412-daba962c6a6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499507 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt[3]{20},$ we first find the two perfect cubes closest to $20.$\n\nThe closest perfect cubes are $8={2}^{3}$ and $27={3}^{3}.$\n\nSince $8<20<27,$ we know that the cube root of $20$ lies between $2$ and $3.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4eb7ee1951d0d402df1553d46bc6e8ce1fdb421adc40949b9e79964484892c6.png)\n\nBecause $20$ is closer to $27$ than to $8,$ we expect $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ to be close to $3.$ \n\nLet's test a decimal near $3,$ such as $2.7,$ by cubing it:\n\n$$\n{2.7}^{3}=19.683\n$$\n\nSince $19.683$ is slightly less than $20,$ we know that $2.7<\\sqrt[3]{20}<2.8.$\n\nNext, we determine whether $20$ is closer to ${2.7}^{3}$ or ${2.8}^{3}:$\n\n- The distance from $20$ to ${2.7}^{3}$ is $20-19.683=0.317.$\n\n- The distance from $20$ to ${2.8}^{3}$ is $21.952-20=1.952.$\n\nSince $20$ is much closer to $19.683,$ $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ is closer to $2.7.$\n\nTherefore, rounded to $1$ decimal place, $\\sqrt[3]{20}≈2.7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499511 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499511 #edb/ref 1069 "q-555461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499511 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499511 #edb/ref 1071 "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499511 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92983ecf-db5c-5c74-b988-756fcd6337e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499511 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $45$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lies.\nWe know that ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${4}^{3}=64$.\nSince $27<45<64$, we know that $3<\\sqrt[3]{45}<4$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $3$ and $4$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3.4}^{3} & =39.304 \\\\ {3.5}^{3} & =42.875 \\\\ {3.6}^{3} & =46.656\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $42.875<45<46.656$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{3.5}^{3}<45<{3.6}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n3.5<\\sqrt[3]{45}<3.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lies between $3.5$ and $3.6$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499528 #edb/ref 1069 "q-555464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499528 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499528 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the best estimate for $\\sqrt{55}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499528 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b73b2161-1f16-50bc-aeef-165cf5e2f96c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499528 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt{55},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $55.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $49={7}^{2}$ and $64={8}^{2}.$\n\nSince $49<55<64,$ we know that the square root of $55$ lies between $7$ and $8.$ This tells us that $6.8$ and $27.5$ are incorrect estimates.\n\nBecause $55$ is slightly closer to $49$ than to $64$ (the distance to $49$ is $6,$ and the distance to $64$ is $9$), we expect $\\sqrt{55}$ to be near the middle, but slightly less than $7.5.$ \n\nWe can test the plausible choices by squaring them to see which one results in a value closest to $55:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7.1}^{2} & =50.41 \\\\ {7.4}^{2} & =54.76 \\\\ {7.9}^{2} & =62.41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${7.4}^{2}=54.76$ is very close to $55.$ \n\nTherefore, the best estimate for $\\sqrt{55}$ is $7.4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499536 #edb/ref 1069 "q-555455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499536 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499536 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following irrational numbers is the greatest?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499536 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c82eb124-d241-562e-a9aa-60dccb777294" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499536 #edb/ref 1074 "To identify the greatest irrational number, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each root lies.\n\n- $\\sqrt{29}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{27}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{40}$ lies between $3$ and $4,$ since ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${4}^{3}=64.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{90}$ lies between $4$ and $5,$ since ${4}^{3}=64$ and ${5}^{3}=125.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{20}$ lies between $4$ and $5,$ since ${4}^{2}=16$ and ${5}^{2}=25.$\n\nComparing these bounds, the greatest number must be between $5$ and $6.$ So, it is either $\\sqrt{29}$ or $\\sqrt{27}.$ \n\nSince $29>27,$ we know that $\\sqrt{29}>\\sqrt{27}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{29}$ is the greatest number among the given options." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499540 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499540 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499540 #edb/ref 1071 "The value of $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between two consecutive whole numbers $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cefb6c0d-04aa-5eeb-98b9-43235f7ba892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499540 #edb/ref 1074 "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect cubes that are just below and just above $30.$\n\nThe perfect cubes closest to $30$ are $27$ and $64.$ We have\n\n$$\n27<30<64.\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{27} & <\\sqrt[3]{30}<\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ 3 & <\\sqrt[3]{30}<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/3e/3efc92b2664e8fade27edc67f977d78b317e73343d3201ac38c1fad807cdb01a.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $3$ and $4.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=3$ and $b=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499544 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499544 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499544 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499544 #edb/ref 1069 "q-555914" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499544 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499544 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the numbers $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{3},$ and $\\sqrt{15}.$ Write these three numbers in order from least to greatest.\n\nThe numbers, in order from least to greatest, are:\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499544 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d53369fc-0d46-5c02-b4f8-5c2f6f6fa8d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499544 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find two consecutive whole numbers that bracket each root. We do this by finding the perfect squares just below and just above the number under the radical.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{3}:$ \nSince $1<3<4,$ we have $\\sqrt{1}<\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{4}.$ This means $1<\\sqrt{3}<2.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{7}:$\nSince $4<7<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{9}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt{7}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{15}:$\nSince $9<15<16,$ we have $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{15}<\\sqrt{16}.$ This means $3<\\sqrt{15}<4.$\n\nFrom these bounds, we can see that all three numbers fall into different unit intervals:\n\n$$\n1<\\sqrt{3}<2<\\sqrt{7}<3<\\sqrt{15}<4\n$$\n\nComparing these intervals, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{15}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the numbers in order from least to greatest are $\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt{15}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499555 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499555 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499555 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499555 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499555 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499555 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the numbers $\\sqrt[3]{15},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt[3]{50}.$ Write these three numbers in increasing order.\n\nThe three numbers in increasing order are\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499555 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eabdbec5-6ee0-5977-9aec-0268ef8c060f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499555 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find two consecutive whole numbers that bracket each root. We do this by finding the perfect powers just below and just above the number under the radical.\n\n- For $\\sqrt[3]{15}:$\nSince $8<15<27,$ we have $\\sqrt[3]{8}<\\sqrt[3]{15}<\\sqrt[3]{27}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt[3]{15}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{7}:$\nSince $4<7<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{9}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt{7}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt[3]{50}:$\nSince $27<50<64,$ we have $\\sqrt[3]{27}<\\sqrt[3]{50}<\\sqrt[3]{64}.$ This means $3<\\sqrt[3]{50}<4.$\n\nFrom these bounds, we can see that $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ is the greatest of the three numbers because it is greater than $3,$ while the other two are less than $3.$ \n\nHowever, both $\\sqrt[3]{15}$ and $\\sqrt{7}$ are between $2$ and $3.$ To compare them, we need to test some decimal values. We can square or cube decimals to find closer approximations:\n\n- Testing tenths for $\\sqrt[3]{15}:$ \nSince ${2.4}^{3}=13.824$ and ${2.5}^{3}=15.625,$ we know that $2.4<\\sqrt[3]{15}<2.5.$\n\n- Testing tenths for $\\sqrt{7}:$\nSince ${2.6}^{2}=6.76$ and ${2.7}^{2}=7.29,$ we know that $2.6<\\sqrt{7}<2.7.$\n\nComparing these tighter bounds, we see that $\\sqrt[3]{15}<2.5<2.6<\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nCombining all our comparisons, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{15}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt[3]{50}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three numbers in increasing order are $\\sqrt[3]{15},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt[3]{50}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499556 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499556 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499556 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499556 #edb/ref 1071 "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt{85}$ lie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499556 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eee5e7a8-3d1e-565b-891d-6f10ecae6e7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499556 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt{85}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose squares bracket $85$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt{85}$ lies.\nWe know that ${9}^{2}=81$ and ${10}^{2}=100$.\nSince $81<85<100$, we know that $9<\\sqrt{85}<10$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $9$ and $10$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9.1}^{2} & =82.81 \\\\ {9.2}^{2} & =84.64 \\\\ {9.3}^{2} & =86.49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $84.64<85<86.49$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{9.2}^{2}<85<{9.3}^{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n9.2<\\sqrt{85}<9.3\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{85}$ lies between $9.2$ and $9.3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499565 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499565 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499565 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499565 #edb/ref 1071 "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499565 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbc69846-f4f2-5c08-841b-d6a9c38b8533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499565 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $150$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lies.\nWe know that ${5}^{3}=125$ and ${6}^{3}=216$.\nSince $125<150<216$, we know that $5<\\sqrt[3]{150}<6$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $5$ and $6$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.2}^{3} & =140.608 \\\\ {5.3}^{3} & =148.877 \\\\ {5.4}^{3} & =157.464\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $148.877<150<157.464$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{5.3}^{3}<150<{5.4}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n5.3<\\sqrt[3]{150}<5.4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lies between $5.3$ and $5.4$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499587 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499587 #edb/ref 1061 834851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499587 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f77a136-9bb5-536c-a5f5-9e3132dc7e39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499587 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499590 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499590 #edb/ref 1061 835631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499590 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62da36f8-cd66-52bf-9d7e-c13cf03ade4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499590 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499592 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499592 #edb/ref 1061 834846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499592 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "650093b9-8f2e-54ea-96a0-c4ce86769990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499592 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499603 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499603 #edb/ref 1061 834841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499603 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9755cb4a-e49f-5626-89dc-85c19b18c155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499603 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499609 #edb/ref 1061 834856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499609 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9f05ba0-f57e-5845-8ac7-3153bd449a36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499609 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499612 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499612 #edb/ref 1061 835630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499612 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b17102b3-2a85-5565-86ee-18a47a1d1472" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499612 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499614 #edb/ref 1061 835629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b59cf68c-8312-512d-83a4-ee45cfcfa2cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499625 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499625 #edb/ref 1061 835633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499625 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dcf6056d-ef27-5b88-b777-e073060226e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499625 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499630 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499630 #edb/ref 1061 835628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499630 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea67ea8f-3960-5050-b5de-d9bafa206de8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499630 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186499436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186499437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186499438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186499439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1031 "Approximating Square Roots and Cube Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1033 492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "76fea41a-98af-5c68-b72e-6881d877a1e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499635 #edb/ref 1064 "When we **estimate** a root, we choose one nearby decimal value and check that it is reasonable. Bounds tell us the interval; the estimate tells us which value in that interval is closest.\n\nLet's estimate ![](images/39/398d865ecee9dba51e8ba4918a187f483b9291ff6ae0d2032be0d95d40190ff7.svg) rounded to ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) decimal place. First, we find nearby perfect cubes:\n![](images/bc/bca4e36ccfb33070d91ecf1f82eab6ce7d8e06f71d05000633a58ac03ce19107.svg)\nso\n![](images/cd/cdf283b9d6e8644576f4e97abe9203e4115c00fd43314a2323706d1c643e9c0b.svg)\n\n![](images/36/3636292deb3377b1dfe99da068f300f7dba4ce7fa6acd22164150451dc396ee1.png)\n\nBecause ![](images/bc/bc98a2fdf686f851fd343e6cca8ebf25186c6d4fe401df573939f8e5e1d5d044.svg) is closer to ![](images/c6/c64ba31f35ed2d1f23f3827854effc6461b82c7cfa7f27694bf545acb565c90a.svg) than to ![](images/f9/f9e10c6abbe30486481349d2f58357e01d37bfd670ed37a29313e8556b57fe2c.svg) we expect ![](images/39/398d865ecee9dba51e8ba4918a187f483b9291ff6ae0d2032be0d95d40190ff7.svg) to be closer to ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) than to ![](images/9f/9fc073d1e6ebde82eb949a875e40be00b9196e9b14473ee13fa47a26a98d8450.svg) Now, we test nearby tenths:\n![](images/47/4703877c1fc434e00d2fc781d77473c9ce8f775257b953203baa76d914c2458b.svg)\n\nSo, ![](images/5d/5dfbe3c8f263be79d4c240a343aeef3ed741dc5d9a72b0b8c21b38cd9548a66c.svg) To decide which tenth is the better estimate, compare distances from ![](images/3b/3b3b7808b8023e6edb41eb64eb15e0198ce6843ff72ec8c44e79786e8932dc84.svg)\n![](images/60/60b754ce1636a8fee75de533c7961f498ac37fd7f6b471971e420985069f3c22.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/bc/bc98a2fdf686f851fd343e6cca8ebf25186c6d4fe401df573939f8e5e1d5d044.svg) is closer to ![](images/92/9252231dcb743cf6f0c6df56fdcc7e3f30a9c4d371ad30332ac7a5f93b38b3e4.svg) the root is closer to ![](images/10/106a07431c2edab74f94a851a8f9d6d4c67fb6e29b7c2090e316e5c2032195e3.svg) Therefore,\n![](images/5d/5d0fdcc02424b0794c647227784e86791079bbdbc184868c2bbbdec3ddcf86cb.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499635 #edb/ref 1065 23017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499635 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "199fa1b4-f88c-51c4-a84f-275792b90dee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499635 #edb/ref 1067 "Estimating Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499636 #edb/ref 1064 "Some roots are perfect. For example, ![](images/38/389d3c3681c9e50a78b79c73049365330340f56d1dd87adfa83ed9cc3e3eefed.svg) and ![](images/47/4788ae8534df07b8199121e142f69086fd3aec0f87f201fcf4997831c0b60002.svg) Other roots are not whole numbers, but we can still describe where they belong.\n\nAn **approximation** is a nearby value that helps us understand the size of a number. To approximate roots, we compare the number under the radical to nearby perfect powers.\n\nFor a square root, we compare to perfect squares. Since ![](images/96/96d11100098752a9a927d8b25751f689094ffee73faec61b29b79e0afa375a42.svg) we know\n![](images/49/49fbd1142d93edad07ad98c73bc1dafb04d1e8b5a4820274f51d8cee4e3dfbd3.svg)\n\nFor a cube root, we compare to perfect cubes. Since ![](images/2d/2de677effff1f2ce5ed73724bfdd3bead3f0baab3bac33ed272af03361ec3573.svg) we know\n![](images/7e/7ec574473402909905dc4960cca1ab56bb5790c5fcd80f4ad78cd3b9954dc925.svg)\n\nSo, the main idea is to find familiar benchmark roots first. Once we know the root’s whole-number interval, we can order roots, refine to decimal intervals, and choose reasonable estimates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499636 #edb/ref 1065 23022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499636 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4dbd01ab-76e0-5e52-b2bb-2efdd00a2058" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499636 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499637 #edb/ref 1064 "When we order irrational roots, we can often compare their whole-number intervals first. A root between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/37/37e17c4a2302e6352fb7186621c9c43bb5e99d43a8a0bc700c0181d36c6cc915.svg) is greater than any root between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/85/85ab4d14e4ae710116ac84de4b03b3cf35723ab969b7abfba97889c497974147.svg)\n\nSuppose we want to identify the greatest number in this list:\n![](images/3b/3b5235ec98b311f2a082eeb8a35ba2e606f452a5467707a2125ce3f27fd26484.svg)\n\nWe first bracket each root.\n\n- ![](images/04/04217800f61f8a2b9dfc2b4efb67a08c1d9f5ef7e365a5ddae4d163146bc0158.svg) lies between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/b1/b1288d1c08b90d72fcd319980da7943ea4941b9f259ece4086d8b24b1bc0cfb2.svg) since ![](images/a3/a34f852d765024d8ae773b8debb02adc77fc371c656b049104a9330dc604f5a4.svg)\n\n- ![](images/5a/5aa880157a445c826aa5e51bfbdf4f3c5ab83afdadf97fc6f533aa6103d28640.svg) lies between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/b1/b1288d1c08b90d72fcd319980da7943ea4941b9f259ece4086d8b24b1bc0cfb2.svg) since ![](images/64/6442e0ee53f3ae690132986dfe60822810ae2f586296bccecba0ab6a07c871db.svg)\n\n- ![](images/39/398d865ecee9dba51e8ba4918a187f483b9291ff6ae0d2032be0d95d40190ff7.svg) lies between ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) and ![](images/39/39c9d5c6b02f72e65180a1a782af0404d25977c3627c5778e1bcb676286506c4.svg) since ![](images/5b/5bf4ba43a56be76e42af12ac6d00913b93191cb348019c518a60bb6490d412ac.svg)\n\n- ![](images/55/556af15807fb8546143a7adb4457089c3f367764397c6cf94654f30200177aba.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/e9/e94ce8a42ab6b97ee9c979dcc0a002b557e1c6339e5950f601a66a9af91f67e5.svg) since ![](images/65/65aec0b2f64e5e7991650650102102099e40d0fa3f33b9951e7f42d02bbba8d2.svg)\n\n- ![](images/ba/ba20e4e917e21ab13b9aab507e09632255fccd6346f08f31333fffd468f3eea1.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/e9/e94ce8a42ab6b97ee9c979dcc0a002b557e1c6339e5950f601a66a9af91f67e5.svg) since ![](images/f4/f4638dd2c251dd734b480e1bcaedeb9e1bee07e2f03ff4c85de10287cae9957b.svg)\n\nThe greatest value must be one of the roots between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/b1/b1288d1c08b90d72fcd319980da7943ea4941b9f259ece4086d8b24b1bc0cfb2.svg) so it is either ![](images/04/04217800f61f8a2b9dfc2b4efb67a08c1d9f5ef7e365a5ddae4d163146bc0158.svg) or ![](images/8b/8bdb2119f2b5ebc9f21dcc182d6b68e8e964156168a62cacb6048ca84b6e4dbf.svg) Since ![](images/eb/eb86e1418a307c988f3b246894cfdf9d0e9c8bbceda1641fb831d30fe9b6fd3b.svg) we know ![](images/45/45ee1b9d94d27812c364bf34c46e918d9c2df4b254adbf86932c2d350cde0822.svg)\n\nTherefore, the greatest number is ![](images/8b/8bdb2119f2b5ebc9f21dcc182d6b68e8e964156168a62cacb6048ca84b6e4dbf.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** We can compare radicands directly only when the roots are the same kind. For example, comparing ![](images/5a/5aa880157a445c826aa5e51bfbdf4f3c5ab83afdadf97fc6f533aa6103d28640.svg) and ![](images/04/04217800f61f8a2b9dfc2b4efb67a08c1d9f5ef7e365a5ddae4d163146bc0158.svg) is safe because both are square roots." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499637 #edb/ref 1065 23019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499637 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "52ad8c4f-2f3c-5dcf-8ac3-f9cfff01832e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499637 #edb/ref 1067 "Ordering Irrational Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499638 #edb/ref 1064 "**Decimal bounds** are nearby decimal values that trap a root in a smaller interval, such as between two consecutive tenths.\n\nLet's find the two consecutive tenths between which ![](images/75/75690cbc152b11d764581bab44be87acabbbf69ccaca10786de7fd41462db7c1.svg) lies. First, we find the whole-number bounds:\n![](images/67/670a358c4cfbfff0330b32c236f83d3cf1b52475f6c624873a54bc335aa11b26.svg)\nso\n![](images/0f/0f2da556fe760c541b67a1f219a28784b92ab589f10c67d8bdefe8d4a27548e3.svg)\n\nNow, we test tenths between ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) and ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) by squaring them. Since ![](images/75/75690cbc152b11d764581bab44be87acabbbf69ccaca10786de7fd41462db7c1.svg) is close to ![](images/3f/3f1007009d0a0d180a52510e7c9ce724113d2deb10051c26452457286845c91c.svg) we can test ![](images/52/521ed5d9c72e7dc13edf7e79df2272294cd869bd5de324f0f7828e2c888c326c.svg) and ![](images/22/227c2d0283023dacad68c1e78194ec0be80b93d52bffea8bd61b5bafc99102df.svg)\n![](images/66/662e926243f31e64daaa74edc74dd4ef9d78e4d24260995fa38db61be6d5cecb.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/e2/e2574f61b59164e433af2aeeb4c0dd160bf84436387873b2af855fa2770b5753.svg) falls between these square values, we have\n![](images/26/26bc3e1ccf6f626db492303aa9d1493c863cf8a10a2e1e46151f1c89ce86c0bd.svg)\n\nTaking square roots gives\n![](images/d1/d1aa32699a90c28680f86d47dca927f92f769ed1e879af7972a3a97030df30db.svg)\n\n![](images/47/4729d848e2e66944d7e2da9f8fc7655a93a59ac5ee20e9454f95b583b39cd8ee.png)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/75/75690cbc152b11d764581bab44be87acabbbf69ccaca10786de7fd41462db7c1.svg) lies between the consecutive tenths ![](images/52/521ed5d9c72e7dc13edf7e79df2272294cd869bd5de324f0f7828e2c888c326c.svg) and ![](images/22/227c2d0283023dacad68c1e78194ec0be80b93d52bffea8bd61b5bafc99102df.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499638 #edb/ref 1065 23018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499638 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "63caa2e6-2c19-5afe-93e1-8e484d252e52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499638 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding Decimal Bounds for Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499640 #edb/ref 1064 "**Bounds** are values that trap a number between a lower value and an upper value. For roots, **whole-number bounds** are consecutive whole numbers that the root lies between.\n\nTo find whole-number bounds, we compare the number under the radical to nearby perfect squares or perfect cubes.\n\nLet's locate ![](images/db/dbd2aff681e9d97891999f08502f4f6e7f41f487b237d6c90e18afa1f77755ef.svg) The nearby perfect squares are ![](images/24/24341571e162e3b4e2ca76590f0ed2e6d8b180365fba30c3fb9ea6041d38179a.svg) and ![](images/3c/3cba1142c4b937d3598aaaed096f8b95178d8c45baf39fb7f9f02f21a21480df.svg) because\n![](images/09/09aad9428a24389e5a19034319e2737d08cf5167bf424e1b78cf0f0e69e49102.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/11/119ef80e819318a00b93e3c34aaca245545c965604be3489dd3529df1232a453.svg) is between these perfect squares, we have\n![](images/8f/8fbdaf9d9bd26df7fe6d1c1ad4403f07ba47a94f1a7f8b0d4e728b6865a7cb23.svg)\n\nTaking the square root of each part gives\n![](images/c9/c9753a4537cde93f9b0a994a0c50bdc6528dba6926bdd021d0cc2b54732277ba.svg)\n\n![](images/44/448579015986d36689743ffeadb6a8b05c96b917778d63a0de7e4a2dd0487370.png)\n\nSo, ![](images/e8/e8219f12c397796f3e761fb39940934f3f6ff51cb7dd3656018be76bcf7db1ba.svg) lies between the consecutive whole numbers ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg) The same idea works for cube roots, except we compare to nearby perfect cubes instead." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499640 #edb/ref 1065 23020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499640 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "914a8dc0-a88e-5f85-bc5d-34aec57c480e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499640 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding Whole-Number Bounds for Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
